Not sure why you mention AoLS here. (From the good ones this
type of amulet is the one I wear least in my games; YMMV.)

And what you write about robes doesn't even closely reflect
the experiences I made in my games. Again, YMMV. Probability
for robes is on the *far* lower end for random generation.
And given that there are generally not that many temples, let
aside that you really want to kill the priest (or can kill
him at all[*]) that argument appears to be quite artificially
constructed.

[*] Sure everything is possible in principle, but that's not
the point - the relation of difficulty compared to other
actions is the key to judge what's easy/difficult.

>
> And I consider attack spells almost essential for disenchanters,
> and any monster you don't want to get too close to on watery
> levels.

As far as spells are very helpful against this type of monster
I agree. Those spells are also very helpful for MF/MMFs or even
sea creatures, if you like. But even if the spells are helpful
(that no one will doubt, I suppose) it's *not* - you say
"almost" - essential.

>
> > The Samurai may like to spend few XLs to become Skilled in attack
> > spells; I'll add that to the "fun" argument. In my own games it
> > very much depends whether I try to read spellbooks at all; it's
> > generally too risky for many character classes (unless you take
> > extreem precaucions, like pre-identifying or blessing a spellbook
> > and reading it on a safe spot on a no-teleport level and having
> > the respective stats pushed to increase the chances to read it).
>
> I don't consider price-id'ing the spell books an extreme
> precaution, and I'll also take A little bit of risk at times.

That's not "a little bit of risk". To value it appropriately
one has to multiply the probability of risk occurrance with
the damage done if the risk actually occurs. Lethality is no
acceptable damage even if the probability would have been low;
and it isn't even low.

That risk taking insta-killed me in more than one game (call
me stupid). If you are aware of that risk it's silly to take
it unnecessarily.

The point with the "extreem precautions" is that any omitted
step may kill you instantly completely "helpless". Feel free
to gamble at your desire. :-)

If a class has to primarily rely on spells there's a different
plan to follow than if a character is a fighter or a ranged
combatant. In Nethack you can stress those roles, but usually
there's a price for it. (We were focussing on Samurai here.)

> But a high XL reduces the risk as well; at XL30, you're almost
> certainly guaranteed to be able to read them all, as long as
> they aren't cursed (and your pet will tell you that). =A0Another
> argument for uping your experience.

That's correct. If you want to cast spells (and need slots for
that) the high XLs will serve both purposes. If your class does
not need to cast spells you can skip that. But to really make
it "another argument" you have to admit that XL increase has to
occur before you start reading books if you want to rely on that
effect (or take any of the other precautions I mentioned, and
then we're back at the argument I made). Now if you decide to
enhance XL as early as possible you'll get also all the
disadvantages early - one should be aware of that.

>
